by Colony Spark [https://www.colonyspark.com/]=0A=0A=0ATHE OPERATIONS=0ABRI=
EF=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AISSUE #20 =C2=A0=C2=B7=C2=A0 JULY 31, 2026=0A=0A=C2=A0=
=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AHi there,=0A=0ANew orders are still the record of committ=
ed demand. They just arrive too late=0Ato be the only signal the team shoul=
d trust. Long-lived equipment produces=0Aevidence before a replacement orde=
r, an overhaul, or a parts request appears:=0Arising service frequency, rep=
eated warranty codes, condition alerts, and=0Aoperator questions moving fro=
m =E2=80=9Chow do I run this=3F=E2=80=9D to =E2=80=9Cwhat replaces this=3F=
=E2=80=9D=0A=0AThis issue is about turning that evidence into an operating =
forecast the parts,=0Aservice, finance, and account teams can act on togeth=
er.=0A=0ABill=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0ATHE SHIFT=0A=0A=0AThe Installed Base Is Beco=
ming the Forecast.=0A=0ANew orders describe demand that committed. Installe=
d-base signals describe=0Ademand about to.=0A=0AOne service call is noise. =
Three calls against the same serial number, a=0Arecurring component failure=
, an expiring warranty, and a compatibility question=0Aform a demand signal=
. The forecast improves when those events are joined at the=0Aasset level,=
 not left scattered across the ERP, field-service system, warranty=0Adataba=
se, telemetry platform, and technician notes.=0A=0AThe infrastructure to do=
 it is finally in place, and the market is putting money=0Abehind it.=0A=0A=
Three signals from 2026=0A=0A=C2=B7 1.6 million connected and reporting Cat=
erpillar assets, tied to $24 billion in=0A2025 services revenue. The connec=
ted fleet creates an earlier view of asset=0Aneed; the service network turn=
s that view into planned parts, repairs, and=0Alifecycle revenue (Caterpill=
ar 2025 Annual Report Highlights=0A[https://www.caterpillar.com/en/investor=
s/reports/annual-report/segment-highlights.html]).=0A=0A=C2=B7 Predictive m=
aintenance systems now combine IoT sensors, CMMS data, work=0Aorders, maint=
enance records, and environmental monitoring, and can auto-trigger=0Awork o=
rders, inspections, and parts activity (IBM, June 2026=0A[https://www.ibm.c=
om/think/topics/predictive-maintenance]).=0A=0A=C2=B7 $13.89B in 2026, proj=
ected $23.79B by 2031 for the predictive maintenance=0Amarket. Connected eq=
uipment and AI drive the growth. Data integration and=0Aaccuracy remain the=
 constraints (MarketsandMarkets, 2026=0A[https://www.marketsandmarkets.com/=
Market-Reports/operational-predictive-maintenance-market-8656856.html]).=0A=
=0AMore signals do not create a better forecast on their own. The advantage=
 belongs=0Ato operators who can connect the signal to the correct asset, cu=
stomer,=0Acomponent, and decision window. The winners are changing three op=
erating habits:=0Athey keep a governed serial-number record across sales an=
d service, they=0Aclassify field evidence into a small set of actions (insp=
ect, stage a part,=0Arevise a warranty assumption, offer an upgrade, or beg=
in replacement planning),=0Aand they compare predicted events with what act=
ually happened so the system=0Alearns which combinations lead to demand.=0A=
=0AThe installed base was once the record of what had already been sold. It=
 is=0Abecoming the earliest reliable view of what will be needed next.=0A=
=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AFROM THE FLOOR=0A=0A=0AThe fleet ages either way.=0A=0ABrent=
 Norwood, CFO of Deere & Company, on what installed-base age signals under=
=0Amuted orders=0A=0ABrent Norwood is CFO at Deere & Company. On Deere=E2=
=80=99s Q2 2026 earnings call in=0AMay, he described what the installed bas=
e is already telling the finance and=0Aoperations teams while new orders re=
main soft.=0A=0A=E2=80=9CWe=E2=80=99ve managed field inventories tightly of=
 new equipment and made significant=0Aprogress on used. All the while, mach=
ine hours continue to accrue, aging out the=0Afleet and driving a base-leve=
l need for replacement.=E2=80=9D=0A=0ABrent Norwood=0A=0ACFO, Deere & Compa=
ny =C2=A0=C2=B7=C2=A0 Q2 2026 earnings call, May 2026=0A[https://stockanaly=
sis.com/stocks/de/transcripts/560362-q2-2026/]=0A=0ANorwood is describing D=
eere=E2=80=99s own operating reality in a down cycle. New orders=0Aremain m=
uted. Machine hours and fleet age are already revealing the replacement=0Ad=
emand building underneath them. The installed base can signal the next orde=
r=0Awell before the order book does.=0A=0AThe takeaway=0A=0AThe order book =
is a trailing measure of demand that already committed. The=0Ainstalled bas=
e is a leading measure of demand about to. Both belong in the same=0Aforeca=
st, weighted differently.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0ATHE STACK=0A=0A=0AThe Installed =
Base Demand Radar.=0A=0AService activity translated into a 30, 60, and 90-d=
ay operating view.=0A=0ANot a replacement for the order forecast. The layer=
 that says what will surface=0Abefore an order exists.=0A=0ABuild a serial-=
number-level demand radar. It reads service activity, warranty=0Apatterns, =
parts consumption, maintenance intervals, condition alerts, and=0Aoperator =
questions, and returns a weekly exception view of demand about to=0Acommit.=
=0A=0AInputs=0A=0A=C2=B7 Asset register: installed equipment, age, and conf=
iguration.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Service activity: calls, technician notes, work orde=
rs, parts consumed.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Warranty: claims and coverage expiration da=
tes.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Condition and interval data: preventive-maintenance schedu=
les and telemetry=0Aalerts.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Commercial signals: open quotes and=
 inbound operator questions.=0A=0AHow it works=0A=0AThree layers=0A=0A1. Jo=
in. Every event is tied to an asset, customer, product family, and=0Acompon=
ent. No orphan events.=0A=0A2. Rules. Known intervals and thresholds fire t=
he obvious signals: warranty=0Aexpiring, maintenance due, part reaching end=
 of life.=0A=0A3. Classification. AI reads unstructured notes and questions=
 for failure=0Asymptoms, upgrade interest, obsolescence concerns, and repla=
cement language.=0AEach signal carries a demand type, time window, confiden=
ce level, evidence=0Atrail, and named owner.=0A=0AWhat the operator gets, w=
eekly=0A=0A=C2=B7 Parts staging list by component, location, demand window,=
 and confidence.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Asset watchlist for repeated service, accelera=
ting parts use, approaching=0Amaintenance, warranty exposure.=0A=0A=C2=B7 C=
ustomer-intent queue separating likely repair, upgrade, and replacement=0Ac=
onversations.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Warranty hotspot report by model, component, age =
band, and operating=0Aenvironment.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Forecast-vs-actual log showi=
ng which signals produced a work order, quote,=0Aparts order, or replacemen=
t.=0A=0ATwo paths to build it=0A=0APath 1: Internal starting point=0A=0ASta=
rt with one equipment family and two signals the team already trusts, such =
as=0Aservice frequency and parts consumption. Back-test six to twelve month=
s of=0Ahistory. Ask whether the radar would have identified the demand earl=
y enough to=0Achange a parts buy, a technician schedule, a warranty reserve=
, or a customer=0Acall. Add telemetry and free-text classification only aft=
er serial numbers,=0Acomponent codes, and outcomes reconcile.=0A=0APath 2: =
ERP or field-service partner=0A=0AAn existing ERP, field-service, IoT, or s=
upply-chain partner can connect the=0Asource systems and automate the weekl=
y exception run. The operator keeps=0Aownership of signal definitions, conf=
idence thresholds, planning windows, and=0Athe decision attached to each al=
ert. No alert enters the forecast without its=0Aunderlying evidence, and no=
 AI-classified safety or warranty action bypasses=0Ahuman review.=0A=0AThe =
goal is not to predict every failure. It is to give parts, service, finance=
,=0Aand account teams the same early view of what the installed base is alr=
eady=0Asaying.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0ATHE OPERATIONS BRIEF=0A=0ABy Bill Murphy =
